<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucas Watzenrode</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1447">1447</a>–<a href="/years/1512">1512</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Lucas Watzenrode the Younger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucas Watzenrode was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Warmia from 1489 until his death, a powerful figure in Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> who defended his see's autonomy against both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>. He was the uncle and patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, whom he brought into the cathedral chapter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frombork</a>, where Copernicus later developed his heliocentric theory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode.jpg" alt="Lucas Watzenrode" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%81ukasz_Watzenrode.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucas Watzenrode the Younger was born in Thorn (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>) on 30 October 1447, son of the merchant Lucas Watzenrode the Elder; the family and its name came from the Silesian village of Weizenrodau, now Pszenno. He studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Jagiellonian University</a> and at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>. As a young canon he fathered a son, Philipp Teschner, born about 1475 to Elizabeth Rackendorff, who married Jan Teschner while pregnant; Watzenrode cared for the boy and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> arranged for him to become mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braniewo">Braunsberg</a> (Braniewo).</p><p>After his sister Barbara and her husband Niklas Koppernigk died about 1483, he took in their four children, Katharina, Barbara, Andreas and Nicolaus. He sent the two brothers to study at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Kraków Academy</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> of 1466 the Bishopric of Ermland, once part of the monastic state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, came under the protection of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, who claimed the right to appoint its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The dispute over the election of Nicolaus von Tüngen led to the War of the Priests (1467–1479) and the First Treaty of Piotrków Trybunalski. Watzenrode was elected in 1489 and confirmed by Pope Innocent VIII against the wishes of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a>, who wanted the see for his son Frederic. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">John I Albert</a> succeeded Casimir in 1492, Watzenrode secured the bishopric's exemption from the Archbishopric of Riga.</p><p>Residing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lidzbark-warminski">Heilsberg</a> (Lidzbark Warmiński), he organised the territory's internal affairs, reorganised the cathedral school and planned a university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> (Elbląg). He argued that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> had completed its mission in the now Christian Baltic and should go to fight the Turks. Facing armed attacks from the Order and Polish efforts to curb the prince-bishopric's autonomy, he guarded Ermland's interests while keeping friendly relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Copernicus, after his studies, helped his uncle with administration and served as his closest adviser and personal physician. Watzenrode died at Thorn on 29 March 1512 on his way back from an official journey, and soon afterwards it was rumoured that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> had poisoned him. In the same year the Second Treaty of Piotrków Trybunalski gave the Polish king a limited influence over later elections of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucas Watzenrode” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the royal elections of 1492, 1501 and 1506, and yet were drawn step by step into the kingdom: <b>Lucas Watzenrode</b> took part in a Senate meeting in 1509 as the first representative of the Prussian council, summons to the Sejm went to the Prussian sejmiks from 1537, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lucas Watzenrode</b>, bishop from 1489, brought his nephew Nicolaus Copernicus to serve the chapter, and Copernicus wrote his work on the heavens at Frombork.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-warmia">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.01,18.6&amp;year=1489" aria-label="Toruń on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4966 338 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lucas Watzenrode"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5416 608h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5416 608h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.01,18.6&amp;year=1489">What was here in 1489? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
